00:43:51.700 --> 00:44:07.030
<v Ira Glass>"'I mean, I haven't done half of what some of those people have done.' 'Done?' I asked. 'Oh my god. Open mouth, insert foot.' He laughed at himself, then hung his head, looking totally lost and defeated. 'I didn't mean to tell you that yet.' He started to cry.
<v Ira Glass>“‘我的意思是，我还没做过那些人做过的一半事情。’‘做过？’我问。‘天哪。祸从口出。’他自嘲地笑了笑，然后低下了头，看起来完全迷茫和挫败。‘我还没打算告诉你这个。’他开始哭。”

00:44:07.030 --> 00:44:16.560
<v Ira Glass>My thoughts came flooding, all mostly gibberish. No wonder I have to convince him to make love. Doesn't he love me? Why? Why? Why?"
<v Ira Glass>我的思绪涌了上来，几乎全是胡言乱语。难怪我必须劝他做爱。他不爱我吗？为什么？为什么？为什么？

00:44:16.560 --> 00:44:40.100
<v Ira Glass>Then she goes on explaining about zoophilia and all she's learned, and how she tried to comfort him. And then she says, in a way, people who go through other experiences have these kinds of things. She said, "I wanted to cry. I felt so close to him because of what he told me. I felt I finally knew him."
<v Ira Glass>然后她继续解释兽恋以及她所学到的一切，以及她如何试图安慰他。然后她说，在某种程度上，经历其他经验的人也会有这类事情。她说：“我想哭。因为他告诉我的事，我觉得和他如此亲近。我觉得我终于了解他了。”

00:44:40.100 --> 00:45:08.270
<v Ira Glass>She says that she wrote him a letter. Anyway, she goes on to say how she's come to peace with it by talking about other people, by talking to other people who have these desires. She says, "It took me a while to realize there was so much diversity in the world. I am very glad I had the opportunity to find out. I've also abandoned the idea that he breaks his wedding vows every time he's with an animal. It's not as if he would ever leave me for a horse.
<v Ira Glass>她说她写了一封信给他。总之，她接着说她是如何通过谈论其他人、通过与有这些欲望的人交谈来达成平和的。她说：“我花了一段时间才意识到世界上有这么多多样性。我很高兴有机会发现这一点。我也放弃了他每次和动物在一起就违反结婚誓言的想法。并不是说他会为了马离开我。

00:45:08.270 --> 00:45:36.370
<v Ira Glass>He's told me that what he feels for the horse is totally different from what he feels for me. The horse fills a kind of spiritual void that I cannot. While I am saddened by the fact that I cannot fulfill all of his needs, in fact, I don't fulfill all his needs, I've come to realize with the help of our zoophile friends that there is no way for anyone to be all things to him, or to anyone. I obviously don't have the right equipment to be a stallion for him. Grin," it says.
<v Ira Glass>他告诉我，他对马的感觉和他对我的感觉完全不同。马填补了我无法填补的一种精神空白。虽然我因为不能满足他的所有需求而感到难过，事实上，我确实没能满足他的所有需求，但在我们的兽恋朋友的帮助下，我意识到任何人都不可能成为他的全部，或者说成为任何人的全部。显然我没有成为他的种马所需的设备。笑，”上面写着。

00:45:36.370 --> 00:47:16.770
<v Ira Glass>"His coming out actually made our marriage better, since all the pain we've struggled through brought us closer. Even hearing what he had done with the animals made me feel closer to him, since it revealed a part of him I didn't know existed. He has told me that if I forced him to choose, he would pick me in an instant. I hope I never get that petty, petty enough to force him into a choice like that."
<v Ira Glass>“他的出柜实际上改善了我们的婚姻，因为我们经历的所有痛苦让我们更加亲密。甚至听到他与动物所做的事情也让我觉得和他更亲近，因为它揭示了他一部分我不知道存在的方面。他告诉我，如果我强迫他选择，他会立刻选择我。希望我永远不会变得那么卑微，卑微到强迫他做那样的选择。”

00:47:16.770 --> 00:47:17.620
<v Ira Glass>Act Four.
<v Ira Glass>第四幕。

00:47:17.620 --> 00:47:32.130
<v Carmen Delzell>When I was a little girl, I thought my grandmother, [? Tuesie, ?] was an angel. I really did. My mother, Bonnie, was what she called a career girl. And after she and my father separated, I was left with my grandparents for the first five years of my childhood.
<v Carmen Delzell>当我还是个小女孩时，我认为我的祖母图西是个天使。我真的这么认为。我母亲邦妮是她所说的职业女性。在我父母分居后，我前五年的童年是在祖父母家度过的。

00:47:32.130 --> 00:47:53.100
<v Ira Glass>Carmen Delzell stumbled into this next story after a funeral. And to get the story, I think you really have to understand the funeral. Carmen is from Texas. And the person who she was closest with as a child was her grandmother, who she adored, and who adored her. And the grandma's house was always orderly and clean, versus the chaos of Carmen's parents' houses.
<v Ira Glass>卡门·德尔泽尔在一次葬礼后偶然发现了接下来的这个故事。为了理解这个故事，我想你真的得理解那场葬礼。卡门来自德克萨斯州。小时候她最亲近的人是她的祖母，她崇拜她，祖母也崇拜她。和卡门父母家混乱的环境相比，祖母家总是整洁有序。

00:47:53.100 --> 00:48:16.740
<v Ira Glass>A few years ago, Carmen put together a little documentary about her grandma, [? Tuesie ?], who was then in a nursing home and not too happy about it. And in that documentary, she talked with her grandmother about heaven, about the past. And she remembered what it was like growing up with a Bible Belt, Christian grandma.
<v Ira Glass>几年前，卡门制作了一部关于她祖母图西的小纪录片，那时她住在疗养院里，对现状不太满意。在那部纪录片里，她和祖母谈论天堂，谈论过去。她记起了和一位基督教祖母一起长大的感觉。

00:48:16.740 --> 00:48:58.880
<v Carmen Delzell>Sundays were always a problem for me. When I was little and living with her, I grew to dread the long, boring services, and the way all the ladies greeted me and asked me if I'd been baptized and washed in the blood of the Lamb. As I grew older, I pretended to be sick, so I could stay home and read the funny papers, even though the grandmother said the devil was laughing at me when I ignored the church bells that rang from six blocks away. And of course, the civil rights issues of the '60s suddenly made me aware that there were no black people in our church, even though we had to drive through a poor, black neighborhood to get there. [? Tuesie ?] brushed away my questions by telling me colored folks had their own way of worship, and that they wouldn't enjoy the quiet Park Place Baptist services.
<v Carmen Delzell>“星期天对我来说总是麻烦。当我小时候住在那儿时，我开始害怕漫长、枯燥的礼拜，害怕那些女士问我是否受过洗礼、是否在羔羊的血中洗净。当我长大些时，我装病留在家里看搞笑报纸，尽管祖母说当我无视六个街区外响起的教堂钟声时，魔鬼正在嘲笑我。当然，60年代的民权问题突然让我意识到我们的教堂里没有黑人，尽管我们必须开车穿过一个贫穷的黑人社区才能到达那儿。图西通过告诉我黑人有自己的礼拜方式，而且他们不会喜欢安静的公园广场浸信会礼拜，来打发我的疑问。”

